1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,217
Now, here's something surprising.

2
00:00:03,597 --> 00:00:11,760
In October 1997, someone asked Michael Dell, one of the most powerful men in computers, a simple question.

3
00:00:11,890 --> 00:00:13,780
What would he do with Apple?

4
00:00:14,260 --> 00:00:16,322
His answer became famous.

5
00:00:16,502 --> 00:00:20,163
He'd shut it down, and give the money back to the shareholders.

6
00:00:20,843 --> 00:00:23,971
And honestly? It wasn't a crazy thing to say.

7
00:00:24,251 --> 00:00:26,952
That year, Apple lost $1 billion.

8
00:00:27,182 --> 00:00:31,932
Its share of the world's computer market had shrunk to about 3%.

9
00:00:32,512 --> 00:00:38,797
But ten years later, the same company earned $3.5 billion in a single year.

10
00:00:39,477 --> 00:00:40,898
So what changed?

11
00:00:41,178 --> 00:00:43,837
And how much of it was really one man?

12
00:00:44,217 --> 00:00:45,468
Let's find out.

13
00:00:55,247 --> 00:00:59,079
To understand the rescue, we first need to understand the fall.

14
00:00:59,359 --> 00:01:07,970
Apple began in 1976, when Steve Jobs and Steve Wozniak started a small computer company in California.

15
00:01:10,050 --> 00:01:15,375
In 1985, after a fight in the boardroom, Steve Jobs left Apple.

16
00:01:15,606 --> 00:01:20,014
He started again, with a new computer company called NeXT.

17
00:01:22,094 --> 00:01:25,030
And for a while, Apple did fine without him.

18
00:01:25,210 --> 00:01:29,703
In 1995, it brought in about $11 billion.

19
00:01:30,083 --> 00:01:32,720
Then the floor gave way. Here's why.

20
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:37,203
Windows PCs were getting cheaper and better every year.

21
00:01:37,433 --> 00:01:43,974
Apple had let other companies build cheaper Mac clones, and those clones chased Apple's own customers.

22
00:01:44,204 --> 00:01:49,594
Its product line had turned into a maze of models, with numbers instead of names.

23
00:01:49,824 --> 00:01:54,509
And the new operating system meant to keep the Mac modern was falling apart.

24
00:01:56,589 --> 00:02:01,573
In 1996, Apple lost $816 million.

25
00:02:01,853 --> 00:02:05,578
Customers hesitated. Developers drifted away.

26
00:02:05,808 --> 00:02:09,768
The newspapers kept using one word for Apple: beleaguered.

27
00:02:10,348 --> 00:02:13,625
Apple's CEO, Gil Amelio, had a problem.

28
00:02:13,805 --> 00:02:17,702
He needed a modern operating system, and he needed it fast.

29
00:02:17,881 --> 00:02:21,010
Building one had failed. So Apple went shopping.

30
00:02:21,489 --> 00:02:25,492
Most people expected Apple to buy a startup called Be.

31
00:02:25,722 --> 00:02:30,471
Instead, Apple looked at the company Steve Jobs had built after leaving.

32
00:02:30,701 --> 00:02:37,136
NeXT's computers had never sold well. But its software was years ahead of its time.

33
00:02:37,616 --> 00:02:45,352
On December 20th, 1996, Apple announced it would buy NeXT for $400 million.

34
00:02:45,582 --> 00:02:49,414
Jobs would come back. Officially, just as an adviser.

35
00:02:49,894 --> 00:02:53,790
Apple thought it was buying an operating system. And it was.

36
00:02:53,970 --> 00:02:58,144
NeXT's software became the base of Mac OS X.

37
00:02:58,424 --> 00:03:02,362
But Apple was also buying something nobody had put a price on.

38
00:03:02,842 --> 00:03:04,114
Steve Jobs.

39
00:03:06,394 --> 00:03:09,672
In early 1997, things got worse.

40
00:03:09,852 --> 00:03:17,929
Apple lost $708 million in a single quarter, and laid off about 2,700 employees.

41
00:03:18,309 --> 00:03:23,613
On July 9th, Gil Amelio resigned, about 17 months into the job.

42
00:03:23,843 --> 00:03:30,022
Officially, Jobs was still just an adviser. In practice, he was already steering the company.

43
00:03:30,402 --> 00:03:36,218
On September 16th, it became official. Steve Jobs was interim CEO.

44
00:03:36,447 --> 00:03:41,026
And he kept the word "interim" in his title until the year 2000.

45
00:03:42,506 --> 00:03:47,277
But weeks before that, Jobs made the most surprising move of his return.

46
00:03:47,457 --> 00:03:50,116
He made peace with his biggest rival.

47
00:03:50,596 --> 00:03:57,734
August 6th, 1997. Macworld Expo, Boston. A hall full of Apple's most loyal fans.

48
00:03:58,114 --> 00:04:01,434
Jobs announced a partnership. With Microsoft.

49
00:04:01,814 --> 00:04:10,425
Microsoft would buy $150 million of Apple shares. Non-voting shares, so Microsoft got no control.

50
00:04:11,705 --> 00:04:16,603
It promised to keep making Microsoft Office for the Mac for at least five years.

51
00:04:16,784 --> 00:04:21,128
In return, Internet Explorer became the Mac's default browser.

52
00:04:21,308 --> 00:04:27,337
And the two companies settled their long-running legal disputes, with a patent cross-licence.

53
00:04:27,817 --> 00:04:32,182
Then Bill Gates appeared on the giant screen behind Jobs, live.

54
00:04:32,462 --> 00:04:34,289
Parts of the crowd booed.

55
00:04:34,669 --> 00:04:41,616
Jobs answered them. Apple had to let go of the idea that for Apple to win, Microsoft had to lose.

56
00:04:42,195 --> 00:04:48,438
Now here's the important part. The money wasn't what mattered most. What mattered was the signal.

57
00:04:48,718 --> 00:04:53,724
Without Office, many businesses and schools had little reason to buy a Mac.

58
00:04:53,904 --> 00:04:56,178
With it, the Mac had a future.

59
00:04:57,258 --> 00:05:01,730
Two months later came Michael Dell's line about shutting Apple down.

60
00:05:01,910 --> 00:05:07,235
And when Apple closed its books for 1997, the numbers seemed to prove him right.

61
00:05:07,515 --> 00:05:12,393
Revenue fell 28%, to $7.1 billion.

62
00:05:12,623 --> 00:05:19,719
Apple lost $1 billion, though about two-thirds of that came from one-time costs, like buying NeXT.

63
00:05:20,199 --> 00:05:24,073
So what did Jobs do? He didn't add. He subtracted.

64
00:05:24,553 --> 00:05:28,983
Going through Apple's products, he kept asking one simple question.

65
00:05:29,163 --> 00:05:31,843
Which ones should I tell my friends to buy?

66
00:05:32,223 --> 00:05:34,967
Nobody could give him a simple answer.

67
00:05:35,447 --> 00:05:41,967
So he cut. Roughly 70% of Apple's products were cancelled, including the Newton handheld.

68
00:05:42,197 --> 00:05:44,471
He ended the clone program too.

69
00:05:44,951 --> 00:05:47,631
Then, at a whiteboard, he drew a grid.

70
00:05:47,811 --> 00:05:52,710
Two columns: consumer and pro. Two rows: desktop and portable.

71
00:05:52,940 --> 00:05:55,193
Four boxes. Four products.

72
00:05:55,423 --> 00:05:58,679
If it didn't fit in a box, Apple wouldn't make it.

73
00:05:59,159 --> 00:06:05,914
It sounds obvious now. In 1997, it meant betting the whole company on four machines.

74
00:06:06,194 --> 00:06:13,439
And the consumer boxes were still empty. The first to fill would be a computer for ordinary people, at home.

75
00:06:15,619 --> 00:06:20,582
On May 6th, 1998, Jobs filled the first empty box.

76
00:06:20,862 --> 00:06:24,758
The iMac looked like no other computer on the market.

77
00:06:24,938 --> 00:06:30,135
An all-in-one machine in see-through blue-green plastic, with a handle on top.

78
00:06:30,316 --> 00:06:33,486
The "i", Jobs explained, stood for internet.

79
00:06:33,866 --> 00:06:41,666
It made bold bets, too. No floppy disk drive. New USB ports instead of Apple's old connectors.

80
00:06:41,896 --> 00:06:48,117
It went on sale on August 15th, 1998, for $1,299.

81
00:06:48,497 --> 00:06:54,057
In its first six weeks, Apple shipped 278,000 iMacs.

82
00:06:54,237 --> 00:06:57,109
The fastest-selling Mac it had ever made.

83
00:06:57,489 --> 00:07:04,031
And when 1998 closed, Apple reported a profit of $309 million.

84
00:07:04,211 --> 00:07:07,893
Its first profitable year since 1995.

85
00:07:08,474 --> 00:07:13,436
Here's the part people often miss. The turnaround went deeper than design.

86
00:07:13,716 --> 00:07:18,679
Apple had cut the stock sitting in its warehouses to about six days' worth.

87
00:07:18,859 --> 00:07:23,992
And it boasted that it was now ahead of Dell, the company famous for running lean.

88
00:07:24,272 --> 00:07:30,856
A year after Michael Dell said Apple should be shut down, Apple was claiming to beat Dell at his own game.

89
00:07:33,036 --> 00:07:42,756
The profits kept coming: $601 million in 1999, $786 million in 2000.

90
00:07:42,986 --> 00:07:48,760
Then the tech industry slumped, and in 2001 Apple slipped back to a small loss.

91
00:07:49,240 --> 00:07:53,754
The lesson was clear. The Mac alone wasn't going to build an empire.

92
00:07:53,984 --> 00:07:56,899
Apple needed something beyond the computer.

93
00:07:57,379 --> 00:08:03,707
On October 23rd, 2001, Jobs pulled a small white device out of his pocket.

94
00:08:03,937 --> 00:08:09,732
The iPod: 1,000 songs in your pocket, for $399.

95
00:08:10,012 --> 00:08:14,462
Slowly, a computer company became a consumer electronics company.

96
00:08:16,542 --> 00:08:22,934
Then, on January 9th, 2007, in San Francisco, Jobs introduced three products.

97
00:08:23,114 --> 00:08:28,290
A widescreen iPod. A mobile phone. And an internet communicator.

98
00:08:28,570 --> 00:08:31,549
Except they weren't three products. They were one.

99
00:08:31,829 --> 00:08:33,613
He called it iPhone.

100
00:08:34,193 --> 00:08:38,793
In 2007, Apple earned $3.5 billion.

101
00:08:39,023 --> 00:08:41,639
Ten years after losing a billion.

102
00:08:43,819 --> 00:08:46,200
So, did Steve Jobs save Apple?

103
00:08:46,580 --> 00:08:51,927
He was the decisive leader. But the rescue was a chain, and every link mattered.

104
00:08:52,307 --> 00:08:58,059
The NeXT deal brought him back, along with the software that became Mac OS X.

105
00:08:58,239 --> 00:09:02,732
The Microsoft deal bought time and trust, when Apple had little of either.

106
00:09:02,912 --> 00:09:09,048
Four products instead of dozens gave a struggling company the chance to do a few things well.

107
00:09:09,228 --> 00:09:13,487
And the iMac proved people could still want an Apple computer.

108
00:09:14,067 --> 00:09:19,776
Jobs later said that when he came back, Apple was about 90 days from going broke.

109
00:09:20,056 --> 00:09:24,912
Whether it was 90 days or 190, the lesson is the same.

110
00:09:25,492 --> 00:09:31,052
When a company is running out of time, the hardest decision is rarely what to build.

111
00:09:31,332 --> 00:09:33,223
It's what to stop doing.

112
00:09:34,103 --> 00:09:36,570
Every empire has a turning point.

113
00:09:36,850 --> 00:09:40,532
For Apple, it was a whiteboard with four empty boxes.

114
00:09:43,412 --> 00:09:47,927
This has been EMPIRE STORIES. If you want the next story, subscribe.
